[38;2;124;124;124mto be an element of P(X × X). So we can rewrite as: ∀f ∈ P(X × X) (f is an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minjective function → ∃x ∈ X, f(x) = x).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, P(X × X) is a set of rank rank(X) + 3 or so. TC({X}) doesn't include P(X × [0m[38;2;124;124;124mX). But the Jech-Sochor theorem uses a rank-bounded version: we can bound [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meverything to some V_α where α is large enough.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the way Jech-Sochor works is: given a model M of ZFA with atoms [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msatisfying ∃X φ(X) where φ is boundable, we can find a symmetric submodel of a [0m
